ch them on make them toss it  biggies customer pet peeves as a chef  are people to have allergies they don't  really know the details of their  allergies chefs hate it when customers  order steak well-done we will judge you  silently behind your back  well someone may say they cannot have  gluten they're allergic and then you ask  them if they're celiac and they have no  idea what that word means celiacs can't  have any trace amounts of gluten  sometimes someone may actually be a  celiac and I'll know it sometimes they  may just not want to eat gluten and say  they have an allergy when they really  don't I will never order a sandwich or a  burger you don't know how long that  burger meat has been sitting there even  a slight tinge of gray can still be  cooked and served to you certain salad I  will also not order unless I know that  it's like a seasonal vegetable I think  about what it's like in the kitchen back  there what makes the most sense to where  I'm at if I'm at an Italian restaurant  definitely going for the pasta if I'm at  a sushi restaurant I'm gonna want the  fresh fish kind of basing my orders on  where I'm at and making sure that I'm  ordering what the chef wants me to order  the joy I find in cooking is that it's  extremely therapeutic for me during the  times that it's stressful it's still  good kind of stress or you know if you  have a good team you're all working  together I love watching people's  reaction when they eat something that I  made there's like this sense of  nostalgia and you could immediately see  the memories coming back to them it may  be the last time they had that dish and  being able to see how that evoke a ssin  of emotion is incredibly joyful a lot of  work definitely goes into preparing your  food if you scarf it all up that's great  it's a compliment but also take some  time to ask or listen to the server  explain how the food was made where it  came from that's all part of the process  and it's not just behind the scenes i